# width: 120
# `ui.dock.labels` (src/app/launcher_dock.zig): the bottom strip's two
# forms. `.icon_label` is the default — ` glyph label ` — and `.icon`
# paints the glyph alone in the same three cells a side dock uses, with
# the name moving into the tooltip exactly as a side dock does it.
#
# At 120x40 the `always` strip is row 37 — the editor area's last row,
# `ui.dock.placement = .inner` being the default — and
# the centred run of six three-cell items (`ui.dock.align` is
# `.center` out of the box) starts at column 49: Browser at 49..51,
# Claude Code at 52..54, Codex at 55..57, HTTP at 58..60, New terminal
# at 61..63, the `+` at 64..66 (it ends the run — dock-polish), each
# ` padding glyph padding ` (// changed (railmove): the four first-party
# surfaces are on the strip, hidden chips and all). So the Browser
# chip's glyph is at x=50, and the hover there is the proof the tooltip
# still names it. No click may land on 52..57 — that would start a real
# `claude` / `codex`.
write a.txt "alpha\n"
open a.txt
ex dock always
# The default form: labels, as before.
expect screen contains "Browser"
expect screen contains "New terminal"
ex dock icons
expect screen contains "dock: icons only"
expect screen lacks "Browser"
expect screen lacks "New terminal"
# The tooltip carries the name the row no longer paints.
command view.toggle_hover_tooltip
hover 50 37
wait 200
expect screen contains "Browser"
hover 60 10
wait 200
command view.toggle_hover_tooltip
# A click on the New terminal item's three cells still opens a shell —
# the hit rects moved with the paint.
click 62 37
wait 900
expect pane Terminal
# And back: `:dock labels` gives the row its labels again, unchanged.
ex dock labels
expect screen contains "dock: icons and labels"
expect screen contains "Browser"
expect screen contains "New terminal"
